{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786730569,"nonce":"GJilNq3Gp5NnhhphCQ0WLrCmDx0=","title":"**What is wrong.** Commit `7558177` replaced the replay gate's axis. `oto replay` no longer asks how old a batch is; it asks whether anything the batch would touch has moved on since the batch was received — `supersededBy` (`internal/ingestion/service/replay.go:262-264`) compares each touched alert's latest occurrence against `batch.ReceivedAt` and refuses on either limb. Nothing in that path reads a horizon, a retention window or a key age. The operator-facing copy and the SPEC still describe the rule the gate no longer implements.","message":"Three places state the old rule as fact. `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:901` tells an operator adjusting raw retention that *\"Past thirty days a stored batch can no longer be replayed safely anyway — oto's event-dedupe keys have aged out, so re-processing it would append the timeline a second time. Beyond that horizon you are paying disk for bytes nothing can act on.\"* `:905` goes further and calls the number derived rather than chosen: *\"The shipped thirty days is derived, not chosen: it is oto's event-idempotency horizon, the longest window in which replaying a stored batch still converges.\"* `docs/design/SPEC.md:979` carries the same derivation into the schema — *\"derived from the `alert_event_keys` idempotency horizon below, because past that horizon acceptance criterion 36's replay would append the timeline twice; the two numbers move together\"* — and `:1196` repeats it from the other side: *\"a stored batch stays replayable only while its keys are still claimed (acceptance criterion 36)\"*.\n\n**The demonstration.** Read, not run. The gate: `internal/ingestion/service/replay.go:262-264` takes `keys`, `firing`, `states` and `receivedAt`, and no age. The reason the old rule was already unreachable before this change: `dedupeKeyHorizonOf` (`internal/app/workers.go:538-539`) computes `time.Duration(rawDays+rawPartitionGrainDays) * 24 * time.Hour`, and `rawPartitionGrainDays` is `1` (`:518-521`) precisely so the keys outlive the raw partition — so whenever the payload is still readable its keys are still claimed, and when the partition has been dropped `s.batches.Get` returns NotFound and `ProcessBatch` skips at `internal/ingestion/service/process.go:55-57`. The condition the thirty-day story describes — a readable payload whose keys have aged out — cannot occur.\n\n**Why it matters.** The copy is on the screen where an operator sets `raw_retention_days`, and it is the justification for the number they are being asked to choose: it tells them disk beyond thirty days buys nothing. Under the real gate both halves of that are wrong in both directions. A zero-write failed batch is replayable at any age the payload survives, so retention beyond thirty days does buy something. A two-hour-old batch whose alerts have since resolved is refused, so retention below thirty days does not guarantee what the copy implies. An operator sizing a volume, or deciding whether an eight-week-old ingestion defect is still recoverable, is reading a rule that no longer governs.\n\nThe deeper consequence is that `raw_retention_days = 30`'s whole justification is the derivation, not the number. `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:120-127` makes that explicit — thirty days is *\"the exact width of the window in which a raw payload is useful, and it is derived from another number in the system rather than picked\"*. If replay safety is no longer age-based, the derivation no longer holds and the default is currently unjustified rather than merely mis-described. That is a product decision about what oto promises and what it costs to keep, which is why this is a ticket and not a follow-up commit.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:897`, `:901`, `:905` (the operator-facing story, all three paragraphs); `docs/design/SPEC.md:979` (the `ingest_batches` partition note and its \"the two numbers move together\"), `:1196` (the `alert_event_keys` prune note and its acceptance-criterion-36 clause); `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:118-127` (the derivation the default rests on). The code that made them false: `internal/ingestion/service/replay.go:262-264` (`supersededBy`, the gate's real axis); `internal/app/workers.go:518-521` and `:538-539` (`rawPartitionGrainDays`, `dedupeKeyHorizonOf` — why the horizon was already unreachable); `internal/ingestion/service/process.go:55-57` (the NotFound skip that covers the dropped-partition case). Ticket `539bfdd` and commit `7558177` are the change that moved the axis.\n\n**Scope.** small — three prose sites and an ADR paragraph, with no code change implied — but it carries one decision that is not the implementer's to make: whether `raw_retention_days` keeps its thirty-day default now that the number is no longer derived from anything.\n\n**Done when.** The tuning copy states the rule the gate actually applies — that a replay is refused when an alert the batch would touch has moved on since the batch was received, not when the batch reaches an age — and stops telling an operator that disk beyond thirty days buys nothing. SPEC `:979` and `:1196` no longer derive `raw_retention_days` from the idempotency horizon by way of a replay that would double-append, since that replay is gated on supersession instead. ADR 0024's `raw_retention_days` section is amended rather than left standing, and the thirty-day default is either re-derived from something that is still true, re-justified as a chosen number, or changed. Whatever is decided, the three copy sites and the two SPEC sites agree with each other and with `supersededBy`.","files":null}]}